\details{
The \code{resample} argument decides what the replicates mean.

With \code{resample = "null"} (the default) the response is regenerated as
\eqn{\hat y_i + e^*_i}, where the \eqn{e^*_i} are drawn with replacement from
the model's residuals after dividing by \eqn{\sqrt{1 - h_i}} and centring.
Those data satisfy homoscedasticity by construction, so the replicates
estimate the null distribution and \code{p_value} is a test. The leverage
correction matters: OLS residuals have variance \eqn{\sigma^2 (1 - h_i)}, and
resampling them unscaled under-disperses the regenerated errors, which
inflates the rejection rate.

With \code{resample = "pairs"} rows are sampled with replacement. The
replicates then follow the statistic's distribution under whatever variance
structure the data actually have, which is useful for describing its
variability but is not a null distribution: the replicates are centred on the
observed statistic, so comparing the two returns roughly 0.5 whatever the
data. Measured rejection under \eqn{\sigma_i = x_i^2} was 0\%.
\code{p_value} is therefore \code{NA} for pairs resampling.

Neither strategy accepts a \code{glm}: each replicate is refitted with least
squares, so the family and link would be discarded and the replicates would
describe a different model from the one supplied.

A weighted \code{lm} is resampled on the scale where its null hypothesis is
constant variance, the regression of \eqn{\sqrt{w_i}\, y_i}{sqrt(w_i) y_i}
on \eqn{\sqrt{w_i}\, x_i}{sqrt(w_i) x_i}. Null-imposed resampling draws the
\eqn{e^*_i}{e*_i} from the Pearson residuals
\eqn{\sqrt{w_i}\, e_i}{sqrt(w_i) e_i}, divided by
\eqn{\sqrt{1 - h_i}}{sqrt(1 - h_i)} with the leverages of that regression and
centred, and divides each draw by \eqn{\sqrt{w_i}}{sqrt(w_i)} before adding
it to \eqn{\hat y_i}{yhat_i}.
Pairs resampling draws each row with its weight. Either way each replicate
is refitted with the weights, so \code{test_function} sees a weighted fit, as it
did on the original data.

Weights given to the fit are known, and they are held fixed. On a fit
returned by \code{\link[=fitWLS]{fitWLS()}} they were estimated from the same data, and since
0.15.0 each replicate estimates them again by default
(\code{reestimate_weights}), so that \code{test_function} sees what \code{fitWLS()} would
have returned for the replicate. The replicate is fitted as the model
\code{fitWLS()} was given, without weights or with the weights that model had.
The variance function \code{fitWLS()} recorded is fitted again to its residuals,
with the same form and on the regressors \code{fitWLS()} evaluated, whose rows
follow the rows drawn under pairs resampling, and the replicate is refitted
with the weights it gives. A replicate whose variance function cannot be
fitted, where \code{fitWLS()} would fall back to equal weights, fails as a
replicate whose refit fails does, and the p-value and the interval are
computed from the replicates that remain; a warning gives the number that
failed. In
\code{inst/validation/weighted-fits-size.R}, with the variance function
correctly specified, normal errors, 150 observations, \code{B = 499} and
\code{\link[=performKoenkerTest]{performKoenkerTest()}} as \code{test_function}, the rejection rate at the 5\%
level was 0.083 with the weights held fixed, as every call did before
0.15.0 and \code{reestimate_weights = FALSE} still does, 0.045 with them
estimated again, and 0.045 with the true weights. See the section on
weighted fits in \code{\link[=performWildBootstrapTest]{performWildBootstrapTest()}}.

Weighted or not, each replicate is refitted by least squares on the design
stored with the fit, in the rows of the replicate, with the weights and the
total offset of those rows, the \code{offset} argument and the \code{offset()} terms.
Nothing is evaluated again from the formula or from \code{data}: a term computed
from the rows it is given, such as \code{cut(x, quantile(x))}, the knots of
\code{splines::ns()} or \code{I(x > median(x))}, keeps in every replicate the values
the fit computed, and a variable the formula found outside \code{data}, such as
\code{offset(o)} with \code{o} in its environment, follows the rows drawn.
\code{test_function} is given the replicate as an \code{lm}, whose model frame is that
of the fit in the rows of the replicate with the response of the
replicate, and the rows of \code{data}, regenerated or drawn. Up to 0.16.0 each
replicate was refitted from the formula on the rows of \code{data}, and a fit
whose model frame could not be rebuilt from them so was refused ("\code{data}
does not reproduce the fit"), as with \code{cut(x, quantile(x))}, a term
computed from all the rows on a fit that dropped some, or a model that
transforms a variable given its model frame as \code{data}; so was pairs
resampling of a model with a variable outside \code{data}. The random numbers
are drawn as before, in the same order, so a call made after \code{set.seed()}
returns what it returned wherever the stored design is the design the
formula rebuilt.

The rows of \code{data} are matched to those of the fit by row name, so that
each row is regenerated, or drawn, with its own fitted value, weight and
offset. Rows the model was not fitted to, such as those it dropped for
missing values, are left out, for the observed statistic as for the
replicates; the rows that stay keep the order of \code{data}. \code{data} that lacks
a row the model was fitted to is refused. Data without row names of its
own, such as a tibble or a data.table, that has as many rows as the fit is
taken in the order of the fit. The rows are then matched to the
observations of the fit as the tests match them (see \link{test_results}), and
data whose rows cannot be shown to hold them are refused, as are values
that differ from those the model was fitted to. A model that is not the
least-squares fit of its design, such as a \code{MASS::rlm()} fit, is refused
too ("\code{data} does not reproduce the fit"): its replicates would be those of
another model.

Null-imposed resampling additionally requires the response to be a plain
variable. For \code{log(y) ~ x} the fitted values are on the log scale while
the data column holds \code{y}, so regenerating one from the other and
refitting would take the logarithm twice. That case raises an error;
\code{resample = "pairs"} resamples rows and is unaffected by it.

The warning White's, Koenker's and the Breusch-Pagan tests give since 0.16.0
on a factor with more levels than observations per level is about their
asymptotic p-value (see \emph{Fixed effects for small groups} in
\code{\link[=performKoenkerTest]{performKoenkerTest()}}). The observed statistic and the replicates are
computed here without it, so that \code{\link[=performWhiteTestRobust]{performWhiteTestRobust()}} and
\code{\link[=performBPTestRobust]{performBPTestRobust()}} with \code{bootstrap = TRUE} give it once, for the
asymptotic p-value they report. The result's \code{original}, the test's own
result, carries that asymptotic p-value: on such a design it is
unreliable, and the bootstrap's, \code{p_value}, is the one to use.
}

\arguments{
